  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 PM EDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nThere is still uncertainty in the details for the tonight through\nWednesday night time frame although the envelope of model \nsolutions is narrowing a bit overall as we get closer. Weak short \nwave energy will be moving through the northeast CONUS upper \ntrough and a summertime cold front will sag southeast toward our \narea tonight. With the core of the upper jet staying well to our \nnorth, the upper support over our area looks weak overall. This \nleaves us with a less certain scenario more dependent on the \ntiming/location of the summertime front (which is as usual quite \ndifficult to pinpoint in advance) as well as the evolution and \nmovement of upstream convection and related outflow \nboundaries/cold pool dynamics. That being said, the main two \nconcerns in the short term continue to be the potential for severe\nstorms and also flash flooding. \n\nThe potential for severe storms still looks to be highest across our \nnorthern areas this evening into tonight.  Most of the CAMS show a \nbroken line of convection dropping out of KY this evening but \nweakening as it moves into our area, with some showing a westward \ndevelopment of the stronger cells following the gradient of higher \nCAPE which is a plausible scenario. How well convection maintains \nitself will be critical for our severe threat and is still unclear. \nThe more significant effective bulk shear (around 30 to 40 kts and \npossibly even briefly higher north) will be across the northern half \nof our area , and low level hodographs show enough of a clockwise \ncurve to keep the low end threat of a tornado in place providing the \nconvection maintains enough strength. The primary severe threat \nstill looks to be damaging winds. Our northern counties will be the \nfirst to be impacted and given the expected weakening trend, would \nthus be facing the higher severe threat.  Also, with the potential \nfor a pivot to the west for the stronger storms our northern Plateau \ncounties would be another location that may see a higher threat. \nHowever, strong to severe storms may push well into our area \novernight if the weakening trend is slower than most CAMS currently \ndepict.  \n\nAs for the flooding threat tonight through Wednesday night, models \ncontinue to indicate moisture will be plentiful with PWAT values \neither side of 2 inches for much of the period and CAPE generally \nexhibiting a tall skinny profile which would favor warm rain \nprocesses and high rainfall rates. Placement of convective \nboundaries and eventually the frontal boundary that will move in but \nbe slow to exit will play a large part in location and timing of the \nheavy rain threat.  Hi-res ensemble data is pointing toward a threat \nof heavy rain across northern half or two thirds of our area tonight \ninto Wednesday as the northern convection pushes in and likely \nleaves a residual boundary, and then the front itself pushes in \nWednesday. Both the HREF and REFS show bands of very heavy rainfall \nsomewhere across our northern half, with potential for 3 to 5+ \ninches in some spots tonight through Wednesday. The precipitation \nfocus then shifts further south by Wednesday night as the front \nslowly sags southward. Given the expected slow boundary movement, \ntraining cells will be a concern and this combined with the fact \nthat the ground is still on the wet side across portions of the area \nfrom recent rains and flooding, will issue a flash flood watch for \nroughly the northern two thirds of our area for tonight and \nWednesday.  Will let later shifts look at whether or not an \nadditional watch is needed for southern areas as the threat shifts \nsouth. It is possible that the flooding threat will linger into \nThursday across the south as the front struggles to push south of \nour area although northern areas will likely be mainly dry.\n\n\nThe remainder of the forecast has the basic pattern of upper ridging \nto our west and troughing over the eastern CONUS continuing.  We \nwill see short wave energy moving southeast on the back side of the \ntrough at times that will bring additional showers an storms to our \narea for the Friday through Tuesday period.  Timing of these \nfeatures and the resulting convection is of course unclear this far \nout. Temperatures for Thursday into Saturday look to be a bit below \nnormal, then the remainder of the period through Tuesday will likely \nsee temperatures not far from normal for this time of year.",
